  "def-nagata-invertible-ideal"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "The assertion is local. Suppose $IJ=(f)$ with $f$ regular. Write\n$f=\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i y_i$ with $x_i\\in I$ and $y_i\\in J$. Since each\n$x_iy_i$ belongs to $(f)$, choose $a_i$ with $x_iy_i=a_if$. Regularity of\n$f$ and $f=(\\sum_i a_i)f$ give $\\sum_i a_i=1$, so the opens $D(a_i)$\ncover. On $D(a_i)$ one has\n$f=a_i^{-1}x_i y_i$. Since this product is regular, both $x_i$ and $y_i$\nare regular there. For $x\\in I$, write $xy_i=cf$; cancellation of $y_i$\ngives $x=ca_i^{-1}x_i$, and hence $I=(x_i)$ on $D(a_i)$. The symmetric\nargument gives $J=(y_i)$. Thus both ideals are locally generated by one\nregular element and are effective Cartier ideals.",
   "uses": [],
   "elementary": null
  }
 ],
